1. Introduction
Information technology, represented by the Internet and communication technology, is markedly affecting the social foundation and life forms of human beings ( Venkatesh, 2003 ). As an important field and direction to penetrate the mobile Internet, automobiles will become the main field of digitisation in the future. A large number of in-vehicle information systems , such as mobile phones , satellite radios and in-vehicle applications are constantly pouring into the car, and the IVIS has developed from the early basic driving information to a multidimensional interaction system ( Hao et al., 2015 , Liang, 2007 ). While under this system, some driving tasks that are not directly related to driving or even irrelevant are occupying the driver's cognitive resources to varying degrees, causing driving distraction ( Patten, 2004 , Dong et al., 2010 ). According to accident statistics from the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration (NHTSA), >18% of injuries and 9% of fatalities in 2010 were caused by driver distraction ( Nhtsa, 2012 ). During driving, besides completing the main driving task, the drivers also perform many things that are irrelevant to the main tasks ( Ma and Kaber, 2005 , Liu, 2017 ). However, with limited cognitive resources, it is difficult for drivers to allocate their attention effectively among tasks ( Cooper et al., 2013 , Castro, 2019 , Ma, 2020 ). Therefore, in such a complex information system, how to manage the complexity of the IVIS effectively and further improve driving safety is a key problem that needs to solve urgently in the current IVIS.
The all-over display represents an important symbol in digitalising and informatising of the future automobile. Particularly, 80 percent of the driving information is visually perceived ( Venkatasawmy, 2020 ), so visual support is the top priority of the vehicle assistance driving system. Head-up Display (HUD), as a visual assisted technology, has attracted extensive attention in recent decades ( Xing and Qi, 2014 ). From the perspective of the display technique, HUD will gradually substitute part of the functions of the instrument panel to minimize the deviation of the driver's sight from the road ahead due to looking down at the instrument ( Liu and Wen, 2008 , Medenica, Aug. 2011 ). The content of the HUD system is primarily based on driving information. In addition, some secondary task information, such as music, phone reminders, etc., will also display in the non-important area of HUD. The emergence of HUD is conducive to reducing the driver's distraction caused by reading vehicle information while driving ( Ward and Parkes, 1994 , Grandi et al., 2022 , Choi et al., 2002 ). Since the HUD is placed in the driver's front field of view (FFOV), it is favourable to reduce the occupation of visual, motive and cognitive resources. According to references ( Gibbered et al., 2010 , Wang et al., 2016 , Bellazzi et al., 2022 ), head-up display technology can decrease vehicle crashes by 25%. Augmented reality (AR) is a new type of human–computer interaction technology based on virtual reality ( Fan and Liang, 2012 , Fröhlich, 2010 ). Combining AR technology and HUD enables vehicles to carry both natural and artificial displays at the same time. It superimposes virtual information such as road conditions, navigation, and non-geometric information of real objects (e.g. pedestrians crossing the road) to real driving scenes ( Smith et al., 2017 , Lee et al., 2009 ). Due to its characteristics of combining virtual and real, and real-time interaction, it has played a strong driving role in the development of future vehicle assisted driving in-vehicle entertainment and navigation ( Choi et al., 2013 , Hwang et al., 2015 ).
In recent years, the implementation of AR technology in IVIS has become a hot research topic for major automobile companies. Experts and scholars have conducted a lot of experiments on driver assistance systems based on AR and HUD technologies, and have achieved many results. Park ( Park and Kim, 2014 ) developed a vehicle-mounted AR-HUD system that can recognize driving safety information and project it into the driver's FOV. The recognition rate of driving safety information can reach 73%, and the recognition speed can achieve 15 fps. Changrak Yoon et al. ( Yoon et al., 2014 ) proposed a pre-collision warning system that combines AR technology and head-up display. The system detects vehicles and pedestrians ahead, judges dangerous driving situations based on the detection results, and uses AR technology demonstrations to warn drivers. Bark K ( Bark et al., 2015 ) et al. proposed an AR-HUD navigation assistance system design interface. By comparing the driver's reaction time in two situations with and without AR, the experiment result showed the AR-HUD navigation system can help the driver respond to the turning position faster. Lee JW ( Lee et al., 2015 ) et al. summarized the problems of vehicle AR-HUD systems in display equipment, target recognition equipment, information organization and visual design methods, and experimental methods . Kim H et al. ( Kim et al., 2016 ) used HUD to design a new pedestrian detection interface, and the virtual shadow of the pedestrian was covered by the front windshield in the driver's FOV. The results showed that applying the system interface resulted in better driving performance and smoother braking behaviour. Hwang Y et al. ( Hwang et al., 2016 ) tested the impact of the AR-HUD system on the driver's risky-response ability and the driver's psychological change. Kim K ( Kim and Hwang, 2017 ) verified through experiments whether the AR-HUD system can effectively improve driving safety and whether the visual symbols covered in the real driving environment will interfere with the driving vision. Zhang Huawei ( Huawei, 2015 ) used various methods of ergonomics and design psychology to explore the design of the visual interface of the head-up display system to improve the driver's cognitive load and driving safety.
A comprehensive analysis of the research status found that there are two main aspects to the application of AR and HUD technology to IVIS:①Apply AR and HUD technology to the forward-collision warning system, project the detected information of obstacles such as pedestrians and vehicles on the front windshield, and combine with the real driving environment to improve the driver's situation awareness of risky driving ability; ②Apply AR and HUD technologies to the vehicle navigation system, and the visual navigation information is directly covered in the road environment to improve the intuitive and easy-to-read navigation system. Most of the research experiments focus on evaluating the effectiveness of the proposed system prototype by comparing the driving performance in different driving situations. However, there is a lack of further discussion on the impact of the AR-HUD system on improving driving distraction and the cognitive and decision-making process in dealing with risky driving situations. As a consequence, the main purpose of this paper is to explore the influence of the AR-HUD system on driving behaviour. In this paper, a simulated driving experiment was conducted to investigate the impact of the AR-HUD system on driving behaviour, and we demonstrated a comparison between the AR-HUD group and the control group (without AR-HUD). The results of this comparison showed that AR-HUD technology plays a vital role in enhancing driving safety and driving behaviour, which is conducive to solving the current problems faced by IVIS, and provided a new direction for the future interface design of the AR-HUD system.
2. Method
2.1. Participants
Due to differences in gender, age, driving experience and operating habits, etc., the acceptance of the AR-HUD system is different correspondingly. In general, younger drivers are more interested in using intelligent assisted driving systems, while older drivers are more concerned about driving safety and stability. At the same time, the driver's psychological condition will also affect their driving behaviours.
The subjects were 30 drivers who volunteered to participate in Changan Automobile R&D Centre, including 20 males and 10 females, aged 21–52 years old, including novice drivers and professional test drivers, as shown in Table 1 . All assigned subjects were in good health and mental state, vision (including corrected) is normal, and no achromatopsia or tritanopia. This experiment was authorized by the Safety Department and the Intelligent Research Institute of Changan Automobile Company.

